resents more abruptly."}, {"t": 2, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the presence of a right lower quadrant abdominal scar (suggesting prior appendiceal surgery) and mechanical obstruction evidence (abdominal distension on exam) support the possibility of intesti…", "summary": "The presence of a right lower quadrant abdominal scar from prior surgery combined with mechanical obstruction signs on exam aligns with adhesion-related complications but offers only weak support for volvulus as a rare sequelum."}, {"t": 2, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Do the meal/alcohol-induced symptoms and lack of cardiac features (no chest pain/arrhythmias) provide stronger support for chronic gastrointestinal motility dysfunction over B2/B3 etiologies?", "summary": "Symptoms exacerbated by specific dietary factors and the absence of arrhythmias or chest pain strongly tilt the evidence toward chronic gastrointestinal motility dysfunction (B4) rather than cardiac (B2) or vascular (B3) etiologies."}, {"t": 2, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the patient's chronic symptom duration without acute, crampy pain or radiographic evidence of acute obstruction disfavor volvulus (bowel twisting) compared to adhesions or other mechanical cause…", "summary": "The patient's subacute presentation without acute bowel findings (crampy pain, radiographic block) makes intestinal volvulus (B1.2) unlikely compared to post-surgical adhesions (B1.1)."}, {"t": 2, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Is the nonocclusive vascular model of GI symptoms (weak peripheral pulses and aortic stenosis murmur) consistent with the chronic rather than abrupt subacute presentation of nausea/vomiting?", "summary": "Nonocclusive vascular compromise (B3) could explain the weak peripheral pulses and murmur, but the absence of acute ischemic features weakens this connection for the current chronic GI presentation."}, {"t": 3, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the week-long duration of GI symptoms with exacerbation by fatty meals and the presence of a right lower quadrant scar provide stronger evidence for nonocclusive mechanical obstruction (B1) over…", "summary": "The subacute, meal/alcohol-triggered GI symptoms and a right lower quadrant abdominal scar strongly support nonocclusive mechanical obstruction (e.g., adhesions) as an underlying cause, with the scar specifically suggesting post-surgical adhesions (consistent with Option A). Symptoms are less likel…"}], "deliberation": {}, "interrupt": {"active": false, "reason": "", "required_actions": []}, "termination": {"ready_to_stop": false, "termination_type": "continue", "reason": "[static mode override] An urgent interrupt is active for hypertensive crisis (SBP 197 mmHg) requiring IV antihypertensive therapy and monitoring for organ damage. Further diagnostic expansion would delay critical intervention. — emergency_override suppressed in static_diagnosis_qa mode"}, "turn_budget_used": 3, "estimated_remaining_value": 0.8, "max_turn_budget": 5, "latest_action_type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "diagnosis_readiness_score": 0.3934514707205181, "benchmark_output_ready": false, "static_question": "Which of the following is the most likely underlying etiology of this patient's symptoms?", "static_options": [{"id": "A", "description": "Adhesions"}, {"id": "B", "description": "Enteric nervous system damage"}, {"id": "C", "description": "Impacted stool"}, {"id": "D", "description": "Norovirus"}, {"id": "E", "description": "Twisting of the bowel"}], "seen_evidence_ids": [], "seen_evidence_phenotypes": "{'Increased intraabdominal fat', 'Episodic vomiting', 'Intestinal ischemia', 'Nausea and vomiting'}", "mode_policy": {"benchmark_purity": true, "allow_external_knowledge": true}, "answer_option_mapping": {}, "root_revision_needed": false, "max_tree_depth": 3, "reasoning_ledger": {"anchor": {"hypothesis": "B1", "t": 0, "posterior_at_anchor": 0.486}, "leader": {"branch_id": "B1.1", "label": "Post-Surgical Adhesions